1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Inuit saw — a polar-night
clock.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Igloolik, Arctic Canada, 69° N. From
late November, the sun stays down.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
It doesn’t return until mid-January.
This is Tauvikjuaq: the Great Darkness.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
No sun, no clock. Hunters left a small
hole in the igloo to watch the stars.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,000
An elder recalled propping a harpoon
beside a snow wall, aiming it at the
North Star.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The Big Dipper is Tukturjuit, the
caribou. An old hunter said, “Near
midnight, it rears up on its hind legs.”

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
By morning, the caribou had shifted
across the sky. The harpoon still
pointed at the star.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Before dawn, the key clock stars rise in
the northeast: Altair and Tarazed,
called Aagjuuk.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Children were sent out before dawn to
check. When adults said aagjuliqtuq,
“Aagjuuk has risen,” work began.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
For older Inuit, its first dawn sighting
meant the solstice. In December 1990, an
elder announced it on Igloolik radio.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
In northern mid-latitudes, catch Aagjuuk
after dark, before it sets.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
In many Inuit dialects, “tomorrow” is
qauppat: “if it becomes day.”

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The Lost Fifth Symbol. Four beasts guard
China’s sky. Who sits in the middle?

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

